{
  "academic_week": null,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "3d1d98cf-58cb-4f27-8598-4ad80cd0ca25",
          "source_id": "9b992641-0cd4-4a7c-af3e-d1ff18def2a3",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "1cd26f41-4ce4-4ea9-9a0c-73c17263d5cd",
      "text": "**Midterm: Weeks 1\u20135.** This is one of the three main study sections for the 40-minute October 29 midterm.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "7aeba6d6-26bb-46de-a908-a730d2d2b619",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "03721243-47b4-494a-af6e-511ba2b5d33a",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "1. What binary is",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "ce207777-cf97-414c-a874-2df369c4c871",
      "text": "Normal (decimal) numbers use ten digits, 0 to 9. Each place is worth **10 times** the place to its right.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "display": true,
      "id": "89073c70-234f-4f09-ae7c-56bede22ef8a",
      "latex": "352 = 3(100) + 5(10) + 2(1)",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "1a61909e-a7f4-4c41-8d97-df9cac3184cd",
      "text": "Binary is the same idea with only two digits, 0 and 1. Each place is worth **2 times** the place to its right.\n\nSo the places are worth 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, \u2026 going left. After the point they keep halving: \u00bd, \u00bc, \u215b, \u2026",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Place",
        "$2^4$",
        "$2^3$",
        "$2^2$",
        "$2^1$",
        "$2^0$",
        "$2^{-1}$",
        "$2^{-2}$",
        "$2^{-3}$",
        "$2^{-4}$"
      ],
      "id": "62eb1bd9-0060-4e2a-ad4a-785624322cef",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Worth",
          "16",
          "8",
          "4",
          "2",
          "1",
          "0.5",
          "0.25",
          "0.125",
          "0.0625"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "c8355654-7405-45c6-843a-461451460e29",
      "text": "The point sits between the $2^0$ place and the $2^{-1}$ place. Learn this row; you use it in every question.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "16b5beda-31b0-4447-a1ab-9ceeb8237f7d",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "80af8d17-712a-4141-bd67-73f2e43bbc96",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "c54c54c6-31ff-445d-952b-e8af2b00e1ad",
      "items": [
        "**Bit:** one binary digit, 0 or 1.",
        "**MSB:** the leftmost bit (worth the most). **LSB:** the rightmost bit (worth the least).",
        "$(1011)_2$ means 1011 in base 2. $(1011)_{10}$ would be one thousand and eleven."
      ],
      "ordered": false,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "edf74c84-27eb-46f1-9160-3f06123ae8f3",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "2. Binary \u2192 decimal",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "ff3a8f12-4f96-4484-8045-621ad844e36b",
      "text": "**Method:** write each place's worth above its bit. Add up the worths that have a 1 under them.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "columns": [
        "Worth",
        "8",
        "4",
        "2",
        "1"
      ],
      "id": "414f023f-88a3-4ef7-90fc-62f8b2775ac8",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Bit",
          "1",
          "0",
          "1",
          "1"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "display": true,
      "id": "58c4d709-3685-4c9e-8c04-0db94c021172",
      "latex": "(1011)_2 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "0f2a04ee-d554-4e6b-824c-604d01705892",
      "text": "Fractions work the same way. The bits after the point are worth 0.5, 0.25, 0.125, \u2026",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "19639fbc-b928-40c9-90f8-84fe43e5f788",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Worth",
        "8",
        "4",
        "2",
        "1",
        "0.5",
        "0.25"
      ],
      "id": "c52a0bf7-a9fb-4584-abf3-99834e867e2f",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Bit",
          "1",
          "0",
          "1",
          "0",
          "0",
          "1"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "display": true,
      "id": "9e0278ca-0069-42df-813d-7112505fffe4",
      "latex": "(1010.01)_2 = 8 + 2 + 0.25 = 10.25",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "60d4b09c-93b0-4a21-9fcf-826721adab4b",
      "text": "The formula sheet writes the same idea as a sum. You don't need it if you use the table above.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "196697df-0691-467c-932b-e1ae18fc848a",
          "source_id": "f5c156fa-fc59-491b-9d3f-f556b596dc1b",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "display": true,
      "id": "e2fe4fb2-b7a0-4743-8d4b-d39a7233ed45",
      "latex": "V=\\sum_{i=-m}^{n-1} b_i\\,k^i",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "64e191b4-6b7b-4238-9a12-9809f562d046",
      "items": [
        "$k$ = the base (2 for binary, 16 for hex).",
        "$b_i$ = the digit in position $i$. Position 0 is just left of the point.",
        "Positions after the point are negative: $-1, -2, \\dots$"
      ],
      "ordered": false,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "answer": "28, 63, 2730, 6.625.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1ab95c1e-c943-4b45-bed8-846c282f9eb8",
          "source_id": "93f0739f-31e6-41f1-8f6f-b09623073bb0",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "39bdfdd4-fd3c-4325-b311-f1c0c3185de1",
      "origin": "source",
      "prompt": "Convert to decimal: (a) $(11100)_2$ (b) $(111111)_2$ (c) $(101010101010)_2$ (d) $(110.101)_2$",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "(a) The 1s sit in the 16, 8 and 4 places.",
          "latex": "16+8+4=28"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(b) Six 1s in a row is one less than the next place (64). Adding every place also works.",
          "latex": "64-1=63"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(c) The 1s sit in places 11, 9, 7, 5, 3, 1.",
          "latex": "2048+512+128+32+8+2=2730"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(d) Use 0.5, 0.25, 0.125 after the point.",
          "latex": "4+2+0.5+0.125=6.625"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: Tutorial 2 Q1.6 and a fraction",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "e4d5eedc-42c7-4b4c-9b69-bce4fa238622",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "1b708a29-a4a4-469f-abf1-9f7fe47e89bb",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ace0f79d-46e1-4d42-b131-c8cab27b4d20",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "2dc8c0cd-0d1e-45f7-a7ce-3133e67f3466",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "3. Decimal \u2192 binary",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "9bca8e7a-db93-45df-ae18-1edb542d08bc",
      "text": "Split the number at the point. The whole part and the fraction part use **different** methods. We'll do last year's midterm question as the example.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "5ce2c8c3-a343-493d-834e-53c179d1c15c",
          "source_id": "bf2ca805-96f9-49d4-87da-ba6471d6a531",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "33de96b3-d745-45c5-82e8-9f14dfdc38be",
      "text": "**Past midterm Q2a:** convert $14.3125$ to binary.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "fcb590bf-4aaa-4cc9-ab94-2fed65684a0a",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Whole part: keep dividing by 2",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "cccf8982-27b8-49c7-9880-bc04ffddd0ac",
      "items": [
        "**Divide** the number by 2. Write down the remainder (always 0 or 1).",
        "**Repeat** with the quotient until the quotient is 0.",
        "**Read** the remainders from the **bottom up**."
      ],
      "ordered": true,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "5ce2c8c3-a343-493d-834e-53c179d1c15c",
          "source_id": "bf2ca805-96f9-49d4-87da-ba6471d6a531",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Operation",
        "Quotient",
        "Remainder"
      ],
      "id": "73ff53a6-7438-4aad-8bbc-4836a8cc3641",
      "rows": [
        [
          "14 \u00f7 2",
          "7",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "7 \u00f7 2",
          "3",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "3 \u00f7 2",
          "1",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "1 \u00f7 2",
          "0",
          "1"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "5dceb1a7-0437-4a12-995f-5fc5546d3e45",
      "text": "Bottom up: $14 = (1110)_2$. Check: $8+4+2=14$.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "6b38bd61-cd0d-48d9-8ac9-f4dc94f7796c",
      "text": "Why bottom up? The first remainder tells you if the number is odd, which is the 1s place (the rightmost bit). Each division moves you one place further left.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "d91901c1-5e54-4286-b440-71a4b05b565e",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Fraction part: keep multiplying by 2",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "3c2a47bf-d7ff-4b3a-b1ff-ab9635f1b48a",
      "items": [
        "**Multiply** the fraction by 2.",
        "The digit in front of the point (0 or 1) is your **next bit**.",
        "**Drop** that digit, keep only the part after the point, and repeat.",
        "**Stop** when you reach exactly 0 (or have as many bits as the question asks). Read the bits **top down**."
      ],
      "ordered": true,
      "type": "list"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "5ce2c8c3-a343-493d-834e-53c179d1c15c",
          "source_id": "bf2ca805-96f9-49d4-87da-ba6471d6a531",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Operation",
        "Result",
        "Bit"
      ],
      "id": "22828f17-a14f-4323-8268-854a1d53e60c",
      "rows": [
        [
          "0.3125 \u00d7 2",
          "0.625",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.625 \u00d7 2",
          "1.25",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "0.25 \u00d7 2",
          "0.5",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.5 \u00d7 2",
          "1.0",
          "1"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "d6c0a069-e454-4066-8194-2c244423a6a5",
      "text": "Top down: $0.3125 = (.0101)_2$.\n\nWhy it works: multiplying by 2 slides the binary point one place right, so the first bit after the point pops out in front.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "display": true,
      "id": "fd9729dc-3445-43d3-8414-1d5e788e0699",
      "latex": "14.3125 = (1110.0101)_2",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "3d1edfcb-bb7b-4592-8d15-36f6d2eb1b3f",
      "text": "**Always check** by going back: $8+4+2+0.25+0.0625 = 14.3125$ \u2713",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "10100, 1100100, 10000001, 10111.011.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1ab95c1e-c943-4b45-bed8-846c282f9eb8",
          "source_id": "93f0739f-31e6-41f1-8f6f-b09623073bb0",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "645511c3-b10b-4acb-8c45-02c66c92b503",
      "origin": "source",
      "prompt": "Convert to binary: (a) $20$ (b) $100$ (c) $129$ (d) $23.375$",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "(a) Remainders 0,0,1,0,1, read bottom up.",
          "latex": "20=(10100)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(b) Remainders 0,0,1,0,0,1,1, read bottom up.",
          "latex": "100=(1100100)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(c) $129 = 128 + 1$, so only the 128 and 1 places get a 1.",
          "latex": "129=(10000001)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "(d) Whole part: $23 = 16+4+2+1$. Fraction: $0.375 \\times 2 = 0.75$ \u2192 0, $0.75 \\times 2 = 1.5$ \u2192 1, $0.5\\times 2=1.0$ \u2192 1.",
          "latex": "23.375=(10111.011)_2"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: Tutorial 2 Q1.1 and a lecture fraction",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "9107aa4b-89c8-4991-a402-689b588a6bb1",
      "text": "**Quick check trick:** for a small number, find the biggest place worth that fits, subtract it, and repeat. $23$: take 16 (7 left), take 4 (3 left), take 2 (1 left), take 1. So the 16, 4, 2, 1 places are 1: $10111$.",
      "tone": "tip",
      "type": "callout"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "687c3afe-9856-467e-a6f2-19b7daa12bab",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "5edd7757-cba8-437b-bef2-062747371e01",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "4. Extras: fractions that never end, and how many bits",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "6eddd3df-7897-4eb7-ae5e-48cff8194756",
      "text": "What if the fraction never reaches 0? Try $0.2$:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "687c3afe-9856-467e-a6f2-19b7daa12bab",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "columns": [
        "Operation",
        "Result",
        "Bit"
      ],
      "id": "726b230c-9c8a-4cbd-8303-e4e6c0293a7c",
      "rows": [
        [
          "0.2 \u00d7 2",
          "0.4",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.4 \u00d7 2",
          "0.8",
          "0"
        ],
        [
          "0.8 \u00d7 2",
          "1.6",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "0.6 \u00d7 2",
          "1.2",
          "1"
        ],
        [
          "0.2 \u00d7 2",
          "\u2026",
          "repeats"
        ]
      ],
      "type": "table"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "a5830f2b-4d46-4566-836a-b687ee671bbe",
      "text": "We're back at $0.2$, so the bits repeat forever: $0.2 = (.001100110011\\ldots)_2$.\n\n**Rule:** a fraction ends in binary only if its bottom number is a power of 2. $\\tfrac38$ ends ($8=2^3$). $\\tfrac15$ and $\\tfrac23$ never end.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$(.1010)_2 = 0.625$, which is about $0.0417$ below $2/3$.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "5ce2c8c3-a343-493d-834e-53c179d1c15c",
          "source_id": "bf2ca805-96f9-49d4-87da-ba6471d6a531",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "6f8a333a-b8c1-47c2-a2d1-c03f72c94396",
      "prompt": "Write $2/3$ in binary to four places. Convert that back to decimal. How close is it to $2/3$?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Multiply by 2 four times. The fraction bounces between 0.666\u2026 and 0.333\u2026",
          "latex": "1.333\\to1,\\quad 0.666\\to0,\\quad 1.333\\to1,\\quad 0.666\\to0"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Keep the four bits.",
          "latex": "\\tfrac23 \\approx (.1010)_2"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Convert back: the 1s are in the 0.5 and 0.125 places.",
          "latex": "0.5+0.125=0.625"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Subtract to get the error.",
          "latex": "0.6667-0.625\\approx0.0417"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Past midterm Q2b: how close do four bits get?",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "b31997b0-1fa2-44e7-8f8d-310143bf0cc4",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "How many bits do I need?",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "442d555b-061f-4562-aa35-ab8cd1f374c1",
      "text": "With $n$ bits you can count from $0$ to $2^n-1$. So find the **first power of 2 that is bigger** than your number. That power is your bit count.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1ab95c1e-c943-4b45-bed8-846c282f9eb8",
          "source_id": "93f0739f-31e6-41f1-8f6f-b09623073bb0",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "display": true,
      "id": "e8ea418c-04a4-4c37-a069-68d5e60af465",
      "latex": "1029:\\quad 2^{10}=1024 \\le 1029 < 2048=2^{11}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 11\\text{ bits}",
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "1ab95c1e-c943-4b45-bed8-846c282f9eb8",
          "source_id": "93f0739f-31e6-41f1-8f6f-b09623073bb0",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "7f7ca22f-406a-428d-bce7-58ec64b27c61",
      "text": "Tutorial 2 Q1.8 for practice: $270$ \u2192 9 bits (512), $520$ \u2192 10 bits (1024), $780$ \u2192 10 bits (1024).",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "7e4c9ea0-9451-4ac0-8b7e-6f03ba35783a",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
